## Borohydride-containing coordination polymers: synthesis, air stability and dehydrogenation
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x www.rsc.org/
Control of the reactivity of hydride $(H^{-})$ in crystal structures has been a challenge because of its strong electron-donating ability and reactivity with protic species. For metal borohydrides, the dehydrogenation activity and air stability are in a trade-off, and control of the reactivity of $B H_{4}^{-}$ has been demanded. For the purpose, we synthesize a series of $B H_{4}^{-}$ -based coordination polymers/metal–organic frameworks. The reactivity of $B H_{4}^{-}$ in the structures is regulated by coordination geometry and neighboring ligands, and one of the compounds $[Z n(B H_{4})_{2}|$ (dipyridylpropane)] exhibits both high dehydrogenation reactivity $(1.4~\mathrm{wt\%}$ at $\mathbf{179^{\circ}C})$ and high air stability $(50R H\%$ at $25^{\circ}C$ , 7 days). Single crystal X-ray diffraction analysis reveals that $H^{\delta+\ldots}H^{\delta-}$ dihydrogen interactions and close packing of hydrophobic ligands are the key for the reactivity and stability. The dehydrogenation mechanism is investigated by temperature-programmed desorption, in situ synchrotron PXRD and solid-state NMR.
Controlling reactivity of hydride-based ions in solid state is important for the hydrogen-related functions such as hydrogen $(\mathsf{H}_{2})$ storage, heterogeneous hydrogenation catalysis and ion conduction.1-3 Hydride-based ions include hydride $(H^{-})$ and molecular ions such as borohydride $(B H_{4}^{-})$ and alanate $(A l H_{4}^{-})$ , and they exhibit a strong electron-donating ability.4 The reactive character causes high sensitivity to moisture in the air, and it has been a challenge to design dual properties of high reactivity of hydrides and air stability in solid state. For example, the dehydrogenation reactivity and air stability are in a tradeoff for $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ in metal borohydrides (MBHs). Air-stable $N a B H_{4}$ releases ${\sf H}_{2}$ above $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C},$ whereas $A l(B H_{4})_{3}$ is pyrophoric with contact with moisture in the air, and it releases ${\sf H}_{2}$ at $60\ ^{\circ}{\mathsf C}.^{5,6}$ The properties of MBHs are affected by some parameters of metal ions – electronegativity and ionic radius.7 A number of studies to tune the properties of MBHs were made (e.g., mixed metal ion MBHs,8-10 hybridization with organic polymers,11 nano-confinement in porous scaffolds12, 13). The reactivity of $B H_{4}^{-}$ in crystal structure depends on hydrogen–hydrogen interactions (e.g., hydrogen bonding, dihydrogen bonding).14, 15 For the precise tuning of these interactions, we applied coordination polymers (CPs) and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs).16-19 Tuning the coordination geometry and reactivity of metal ions or bridging ligands can afford the precise configuration of ions/molecules in CP architectures. CP structures are thought to be constructed with $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ ; however it has been unsuccessful because of the high reactivity of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ . The attempts to incorporate reactive $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ into CP structures readily proceed the reduction of metal ions or decomposition of organic ligands. There are many reports on the polymeric crystal structures consist of metal ions and bridging $B H_{4}^{-}$ −.20 On the other hand, the extended $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ -based crystal structures constructed from metal ions and organic bridging ligands are limited: $(\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{M g}(\mathsf{B H}_{4})_{2}(\mathsf{p y r a z i n e})_{2}]$ and $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{T h}(\mathsf{O T e r}^{\mathsf{M e s}})_{2}(\mathsf{B H}_{4})_{2}(4,4^{\prime}-$ bipyridyl)]) were only reported from the Cambridge Crystallographic Data Centre (CCDC) database.21, 22 As shown in the previous studies, neutral N-donor ligands are suitable to incorporate $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ as counter anions in CP structures. We then used commercially available $M g(B H_{4})_{2}$ and $C a(B H_{4})_{2}$ for CP synthesis using neutral N-donor ligands under Ar. The solution reaction using $M g(B H_{4})_{2}$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridyl in acetonitrile (MeCN) afforded a polymeric structure. $C a(B H_{4})_{2}$ with poor solubility to organic solvents is not suitable solution reactions. Solvent-free mechanochemical reaction of $C a(B H_{4})_{2}$
and pyrazine produced crystalline powder of CP. On the other hand, the synthetic attempts using other pyridyl-based ligands afforded amorphous powder or no solid product. This is because of a weak coordination interaction between hard ${\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{g}}^{2+}$ or $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ and soft N-donor ligands according to the Hard and Soft Acids and Bases (HSAB) theory. The theory tells soft transition metal ions (e.g., $Z n^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}$ ) form stable coordination bonds with N-donor ligands. $[P P h_{4}Z n(B H_{4})_{3}]$ and $[M n(B H_{4})_{2}$ ·3THF] $N a B H_{4}$ were therefore prepared as starting materials.23 Bulky ${\mathsf{P P h}}_{4}^{+}$ cation was incorporated to increase the solubility of the salt. Transition metal ions-based MBHs precursor successfully form coordination bonds with pyridyl and imidazolate ligands, and $10\ {\mathsf{C P}}$ crystals were isolated as summarized in Figure 1. In general, the structural analysis from single-crystal for MBH is difficult, and usually hard to tell the exact position of hydrogen atoms in $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ .24, 25 In the present cases, high-quality single-crystal diffraction data enables to locate hydrogen atoms of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ in the Fourier map and refine freely without restrain. This is beneficial to understand the local structure of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ and resulting properties.
As shown in Figure 1, each product is classified according to the local coordination environments at $\mathsf{M}^{2+}$ center (tetrahedral, trigonal bi-pyramidal and octahedral, where $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ are assumed to occupy one site). As the coordination number of ligands increases, the M–B distance increases (tetrahedral: 2.339 Å, trigonal bi-pyramidal: 2.499 $\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ octahedral: $2.696\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , Table S3). Electron-donation from N-donor ligands to metal ions decreases the electronic interaction between $M-B H_{4}^{-}$ .21 Coordination mode of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ to metal ions was clearly determined, and most of them were bidentate. In addition, $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ in the structures show dihydrogen bonding with hydrogen atoms of bridging ligands. Dihydrogen bond is an interaction between negatively-charged hydrogen and positively-charged hydrogen (e.g., $\mathsf{B{-}H}^{\delta-}{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}{\boldsymbol{\cdot}}\mathsf{H}^{\delta+}{\boldsymbol{-}}\mathsf{C},$ normally $<2.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}\}$ , and affects the crystal packing and physical properties.26 The number of dihydrogen bonds varies in the range of 0 to 3 as summarized in Table S3. Four packing structures were observed, one– dimensional (1D) chain (1, 2, 3, 4), 1D ladder (5, 6, 7, 8), two– dimensional (2D) bilayer (9) and 2D sheet (10). The CPs with a higher coordination number of the ligands show higher structural dimensionality. 5, 6, 7 and 9 contain MeCN as guest molecules, and the permanent porosity of 5 and 6 was determined by ${\sf N}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption (Figure S27–28).
Bulk powder samples were prepared under the same reaction conditions, and phase purity was confirmed by PXRD and infrared (IR) spectroscopy under Ar (Figure S24–25). The thermal properties were characterized by thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) under Ar (Figure S26). As various types of chemical environments of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ were observed in 1–10, the dependence of the air stability on the local structure was examined. Each powder sample $\left(30{\mathrm{~mg}}\right)$ was exposed to the humidified air (relative humidity (RH) $50\%$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ Figure S29), and time-course IR spectra and PXRD patterns were collected to monitor $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ . Reference MBHs, $N a B H_{4}$ and $M g(B H_{4})_{2}$ were also evaluated for comparison. After air exposure of $M g(B H_{4})_{2}$ for 3 hours, no peak is observed in the $B-H$ region in the IR spectrum (Figure 2A). The broaden peak at $3500~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ indicates the hydrolysis of $B H_{4}^{-}$ . Although $N a B H_{4}$ is considered as an airstable MBH, it showed deliquescence in 2 hours at the condition and hydrolyzed to form $\mathsf{N a}_{2}[\mathsf{B}_{4}\mathsf{O}_{5}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}]\cdot3\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ after the air exposure for 7 days (Figure S30). The humidified condition is enough harsh to hydrolyze $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ in most of MBHs.
Some crystalline powder samples changed to amorphous in a few minutes (5, ${\mathsf{M}}{\mathsf{g}}^{2+}$ and 10, $\mathsf{C a}^{2+}$ ) or 1 day (3–4, 6–9, $\mathsf{M n}^{2+})$ .
Notably, among the $B H_{4}^{-}-C P S$ , $Z n^{2+}$ -based CPs (1, 2) exhibited high air stability. In particular, 2 exhibits IR spectra identical before and after the air exposure for 7 days in Figure 2B. In addition, 2 retains highly crystalline peaks in PXRD (Figure 2B, inset). The results demonstrate improved air stability of 2, and the mechanisms are discussed based on the crystal structure of 2. Although many structural parameters affect the air stability, we classify the main contributions to the high air stability of 2 as (i) metal–ligand coordination bond (ii) dihydrogen bonding and (iii) packing structure. 2 shows higher air stability than the isostructural $\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{2+}$ -based 3. In general, a metal–ligand bond is an essential parameter to determine the air stability of CPs.27 The $z_{n-N}$ bond in tetrahedral geometry is known to construct highly stable CPs.28 Meanwhile, limited examples of CPs with the Mn–N in tetrahedral geometry have been reported, and most of them are sensitive to the air.29, 30 The tendency of air stability depending on metal ions is also suggested by the survey on the CCDC database.31 Figure 3C displays the dihydrogen bond between $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ and the neighboring dipyridylpropane (dpp) in the structure of 2. In addition to the X-ray diffraction analysis, the geometry optimization on 2 utilizing DFT calculation suggests dihydrogen bonds form as well (Figure S46). In general, dihydrogen bonding stabilizes a crystal structure by the electrostatic interaction between oppositely charged hydrogen atoms,32, 33 and the interaction lowers the electron donating ability of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ . Intermolecular interaction and packing structure are also essential for the air stability, because they affect the diffusion of $H_{2}O$ molecules in the structure. $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ are surrounded by the hydrophobic propyl group $(2.545-3.010\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ as shown in Figure 2D, which avoids the attack of $H_{2}O$ . In addition, the concentration of hydrophobic species around $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ of 2 was compared with other CPs showing different air stability. The number of carbon atoms away from the boron atoms of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ within $5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ was counted for 2, 9 and 10 (Figure S47). 2 shows a higher concentration of carbon atoms than that of 9 and 10 (total numbers of carbon atoms within $5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , 2: 47, 9: 39, 10: 16), which also contributes to the high air stability of 2.34
As 2 exhibited exceptionally high air stability, the dehydrogenation property of 2 was characterized. TGA profile under Ar exhibits a characteristic weight loss of $1.4\ \mathsf{w t}\%$ at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a second weight loss at $260~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ (Figure S26). The released chemical species at $170^{\circ}\mathsf C$ were analyzed using temperature-programmed desorption (TPD) as shown in Figure 3A. The release of ${\sf H}_{2}$ $(m z=2)$ ) starts from $108^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ and peaks are observed at 179 and $203^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in the TPD profile, corresponding to the weight loss $(1.4~\mathrm{wt\%})$ at $170~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ by TGA. The observed dehydrogenation temperature for 2 is higher than that of $\mathsf{\left[N a Z n(B H_{4})_{3}\right](103\ ^{\circ}C),^{\beta}}$ which is due to the stabilization effect of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ in 2. In spite of the higher air stability of 2 than $N a B H_{4},$ 2 exhibits the lower dehydrogenation temperature than $N a B H_{4}$ (170 vs. $505~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . Note that dpp ligands decreases the weight amount of $\mathsf{H}_{2},$ and the $1.4~\mathsf{w t\%}$ is smaller than that of most of MBHs. The close contact of each $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ enables the dehydrogenation at a lower temperature. The inset of Figure 3A displays the closest distance between $B{\mathrm{-}}H\cdot\cdot\cdot H-B$ (2.645 Å), which is comparable to that of $Z n^{2+}$ -based MBHs $([\mathsf{L i Z n}_{2}(\mathsf{B H}_{4})_{5}].$ ; $2.482\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ , $[N a Z n(B H_{4})_{3}]$ ; 2.291 Å).8 Tetrahedral geometry in 2 is suitable to arrange $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ in a close proximity. The variable temperature synchrotron PXRD experiments indicate the structural expansion of 2 upon heating $(25-226^{\circ}C,$ Figure S38– 39). 2 does not exhibit an amorphous phase before dehydrogenation at $179^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , and metallic Zn peaks are observed above ${180~^{\circ}C}$ . The dehydrogenation of two $B H_{4}^{-}$ produces one molecule of ${\sf H}_{2}$ and subsequently reduces $Z n^{2+}$ to metallic Zn.
The environment of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ after the dehydrogenation was characterized by solid-state $^{11}{\mathsf{B}}$ magic angle spinning (MAS) nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR). $^{11}{\mathsf{B}}$ NMR spectrum of the pristine 2 shows two peaks at $-45$ and $^{-47}$ ppm, and both peaks correspond to crystallographically independent $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ (Figure 3B, inset).8 The dehydrogenized 2 displays $^{11}{\mathsf{B}}$ peaks at $^{-8}$ , 0, 15, and 30 ppm in Figure 3B, and were further characterized by 2D multi quantum (MQ) MAS NMR spectrum (Figure S42). To identify the resultant boron species, 2D $\mathsf{\Omega}^{1}\mathsf{H}\mathrm{-}\bigl\{\mathsf{\Omega}^{11}\mathsf{B}\bigr\}$ through-bond heteronuclear multiple quantum coherence (HMQC) experiment was performed (Figure S43). The $^{11}{\mathsf{B}}$ peaks observed at $^{-8}$ and 0 ppm are correlated with the proton at 2 ppm, indicative $B-H$ bond. These boron species are assigned to the $[({\mathsf{B H}}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{d p p}]$ complexes.35 Meanwhile, the remaining two $^{11}{\mathsf{B}}$ peaks (15, 30 ppm) do not exhibit a clear correlation, indicating no direct bond between these boron species and hydrogen atoms. The $^{11}{\mathsf{B}}$ peak at 15 ppm corresponds to elemental boron, and matches well with the simulated one (Figure S44), while the $^{11}\mathsf{B}$ peak at 30 ppm corresponds to B–3N species (e.g., $[\mathsf{B}(\mathsf{d}\mathsf{p}\mathsf{p})_{x}])$ .36-38 The 2D $^1{\mathsf{H}}/{}^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ heteronuclear correlation spectrum clearly exhibits the $^1\mathsf{H}$ and $^{13}{\mathsf{C}}$ peaks corresponding to the aromatic ring and aliphatic chain of dpp, indicating that dpp does not decompose during dehydrogenation (Figure S45). The first peak of ${\sf H}_{2}$ release at ${179^{\circ}}\mathsf C$ in TPD corresponds to the dehydrogenation of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ (Eq. 1). The release of toxic $\mathsf{B}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}$ is suppressed by the complexation with the N-donor dpp ligand to form $[({\mathsf{B H}}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{d p p}]$ as revealed by solid-state NMR and IR spectra (Figure S40). The second peak of ${\sf H}_{2}$ release at $203^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in TPD corresponds to the further dehydrogenation of $[({\mathsf{B H}}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{d p p}]$ . Solid-state NMR suggests that $[({\mathsf{B H}}_{3})_{2}\mathsf{d p p}]$ partly forms elemental boron and B–3N species such as $[\mathsf{B}(\mathsf{d}\mathsf{p}\mathsf{p})_{x}]$ through the dehydrogenation (Eq. 2). 2 arranged $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ and nitrogen atoms of dpp close to each other in a 1:1 ratio $(B\cdots N$ distance; 3.450, $3.462\mathrm{~\AA~}$ Figure S35). 2 maintains the crystal structure before dehydrogenation at ${179^{\circ}}\mathsf C$ as observed by in situ PXRD. The results indicate that the pre-organized environment of $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ in 2 is preserved before the dehydrogenation, leading to the release of pure ${\sf H}_{2}$ without $B_{2}\mathsf{H}_{6}$ .
## Conclusions
By considering suitable combinations of metal ions and N-donor ligands, we have constructed 10 coordination polymer crystals involving reactive $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ with various coordination geometries. In particular, $[Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{B}\mathsf{H}_{4})_{2}$ (dipyridylpropane)] (2) demonstrated both high dehydrogenation reactivity and high air stability. The crystal structure of 2 was intact under the humidified condition (50 $R H\%$ at $25^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ 7 days) which was exceptionally high air stability as compared with conventional MBHs. This is due to the strong metal–ligand bond, the electrostatic stabilization by dihydrogen bonding, and close packing of hydrophobic group with $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ . In spite of the stabilization of $B H_{4}^{-}$ , the dehydrogenation reactivity was maintained (pure ${\sf H}_{2},$ $,1.4~\mathsf{w t\%}$ , $179^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ ). Tetrahedral geometry in 2 arranged two $\mathsf{B H}_{4}^{-}$ closely, which accelerated the dehydrogenation. We demonstrated that crystal engineering of coordination polymers and metal– organic frameworks expands the library of hydride-based crystal structures and their properties.
